#%PAM-1.0
#
# IRN OS override of the stock greetd PAM stack.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS: greetd here only ever AUTOLOGINS the fixed, unprivileged kiosk
# user `irn` (see /etc/greetd/config.toml -> [initial_session]). There is no
# interactive greeter, root is locked, ssh is masked, and the image is an
# amnesiac live appliance — so greetd's job is to start one known session, not to
# guard a login.
#
# The stock file does `@include login`, whose auth stack is `pam_unix.so nullok`.
# live-config creates `irn` at boot with a DISABLED password (`*` in shadow) and
# only wires passwordless autologin for display managers it recognises — greetd is
# not one of them. `pam_unix` accepts an EMPTY password but rejects a disabled one,
# so autologin failed with "could not authenticate" (post-GRUB, post-kernel).
#
# Permit auth for this autologin-only service, but keep the normal account/session
# handling so pam_systemd still registers the seat and exports XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
# (the kiosk relies on it — see /usr/local/bin/irn-kiosk).
#
# SECURITY NOTE: `auth required pam_permit.so` means greetd performs no password
# check. That is acceptable ONLY because this service exclusively autologins the
# unprivileged kiosk user on a login-less appliance. Do NOT copy this stack to any
# greetd instance that offers an interactive login.

auth       required   pam_permit.so
account    required   pam_permit.so

session    required   pam_limits.so
session    optional   pam_env.so readenv=1
session    optional   pam_env.so readenv=1 envfile=/etc/default/locale
@include common-session
